schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: Onboarding New Members to Your Local Node
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: guide
  summary: >-
    A practical playbook for local-node stewards on welcoming and integrating
    new members without becoming the bottleneck. Reframes onboarding as a
    "trellis, not a funnel" — a pre-built structure newcomers climb rather than
    a conversion pipeline. Centers the first-90-days retention window, a
    one-buddy-per-newcomer system, a repeatable first-week structure, using
    web3 tooling (POAP, USSD access) as a welcome mat rather than a gate, and
    graduating members into roles (observe → first contribution → real role)
    so onboarding becomes self-sustaining and steward burnout is addressed as
    part of the onboarding design itself.
  audience: local node stewards / community organizers
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      Token-gating as a filtering instinct ("prove you belong before we let
      you in") vs. the article's explicit recommendation to use web3 tools
      (POAP, phone-first access) to lower barriers instead.
    - >-
      Leaning on network-level support (ReFi DAO incubator, shared guides)
      framed as "mutual aid, not central overreach" — the article draws this
      line but flags it as something a steward must actively guard.
  related_concepts:
    - onboarding-as-trellis-not-funnel
    - seed-sapling-canopy-node-maturity
  related_resources:
    - poap
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/onboarding-members.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
